WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.620
Where did a virus get a cancer gene?

2
00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:12.640
In San Francisco, Michael Bishop and Harold Varmus shared a laboratory.

3
00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:19.740
A French postdoctoral fellow there, Dominique Stehelin, made a radioactive strand of

4
00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.660
DNA. It matched the src gene and no other part of the virus.

5
00:00:25.540 --> 00:00:29.300
A single strand of DNA sticks to its matching sequence.

6
00:00:29.300 --> 00:00:35.000
So this probe could ask whether any sample of DNA held a relative of src.

7
00:00:35.800 --> 00:00:40.800
The probe stuck to DNA from chickens that had never met the virus.

8
00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:43.280
That alone proved little.

9
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:50.940
A leading idea of the day held that old infections had left viral genes behind in chicken chromosomes.

11
00:00:51.220 --> 00:00:55.980
So a match in chickens could still be a leftover from an old infection.

12
00:00:56.883 --> 00:01:00.743
So Stehelin tried duck, quail and emu.

13
00:01:01.623 --> 00:01:06.103
An emu is a flightless bird at a great evolutionary distance from the chicken.

14
00:01:06.903 --> 00:01:08.803
The probe stuck to all of them.

15
00:01:09.603 --> 00:01:13.983
The matches were close enough that Varmus judged the sequence had been conserved for

16
00:01:13.983 --> 00:01:16.083
at least a hundred million years.

17
00:01:17.063 --> 00:01:21.763
A sequence shared across that distance is older than any infection of chickens.

18
00:01:22.817 --> 00:01:25.597
It was a gene of the birds themselves.

19
00:01:26.437 --> 00:01:29.497
Later work found it in mammals, people included.

20
00:01:30.377 --> 00:01:33.617
The virus had picked up the cell's gene by accident.

21
00:01:34.617 --> 00:01:40.077
A normal gene that can become an oncogene when it is altered is a proto-oncogene.

22
00:01:41.057 --> 00:01:43.977
What the virus carries now is an altered copy.
